            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <ceilings>In addition to reducing energy use, increasing the insulation level of your attic will keep your house warmer during the winter and cooler during the summer. Effective insulation and air sealing slow the movement of heat and air, and help prevent moisture accumulation in the attic. 



When insulating attics, the importance of air sealing cannot be overstated. Before insulating, seal all openings and penetrations to stop interior air from entering the attic.  Seal gaps around ceiling light fixtures, plumbing stacks, wiring, chimneys and the tops of interior walls. Install weather-stripping around the hatch or door, and use hooks with eye bolts or a latch to hold the hatch firmly against the weather-stripping.



Ensure that soffit venting is not blocked by the insulation. Baffles may need to be installed against the underside of the roof along the soffits to ensure proper ventilation.



As is important with the installation of all products, follow the manufacturer?????????s instructions and check with your local building authorities for information on fire safety requirements. Do not install insulation or flammable material against or over heat sources, such as masonry or metal chimneys and recessed lighting fixtures. Consult with chimney experts regarding appropriate barriers to keep adequate clearance to these structures before beginning air sealing and insulating the attic area.



For more information on insulating attics, consult NRCan?????????s publication entitled Keeping the Heat In, and Canada Mortgage and Housing Corporation?????????s About Your House and Renovating for Energy Savings fact sheets.

</ceilings>
                <HeatingSystem>Air-source heat pumps extract heat in the air from outside and transfer it to a distribution system in the house during the heating season.



Different terminology is used for the efficiency ratings of heat pumps. For example, air-source heat pumps have seasonal heating and cooling ratings. The heating rating is the Heating Seasonal Performance Factor (HSPF), while cooling is rated by the Seasonal Energy Efficiency Ratio (SEER) and the Energy Efficiency Ratio (EER). However, in the manufacturers' catalogues you may still see Coefficient of Performance (COP), which is used to rate cooling or heating efficiencies. The higher the rating, the more energy efficient is the heat pump. ENERGY STAR; qualified heat pumps are among the most energy efficient in the marketplace.



An air-source heat pump consists of a matched outdoor unit (comprised of a condenser coil, compressor and cooling fan) and an indoor unit (typically an evaporator coil located on the wall in a main area of the home or multiple rooms). Many manufacturers voluntarily submit their products for testing by the Air Conditioning, Heating and Refrigeration Institute (AHRI) to assure consumers that their energy efficiency claims have been verified by an independent, third-party source. However, the AHRI does not certify individual indoor or outdoor units. The AHRI only certifies matched indoor and outdoor coils which work together to achieve the given SEER/EER/HSPF rating for the heat pump system. In New Brunswick it is important to get a heat pump that works in our colder climate, these are called Cold Climate Heat Pumps. They are able to work even in very cold winter temperatures. Rebates will only be given for heat pumps that are NB Power?????????s list of acceptable heat pumps and only when installed by a contractor that is on NB Powers list of acceptable contractors. See Eligibility requirements at the back of the program guidelines that were attached to your email. If you are unsure of eligibility, please contact your energy advisor.

Ask your contractor to indicate the AHRI Certified Reference number on your invoice, as well as the indoor and outdoor unit model numbers and provide this information to your energy advisor at the time of the post-retrofit evaluation.



For more information on air-source heat pumps, refer to the NRCan publication entitled Heating and Cooling with a Heat Pump



</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>Thank you for choosing Homesol Building Solutions Inc. for your energy consulting needs. You will be mailed your official EnerGuide label indicating your home's energy efficiency rating, an estimate of your home's annual reference energy consumption and your EnerGuide file number.  The label is designed to be affixed to your main electrical panel box for easy future reference. 



If you plan to contract out any work, always use qualified, trained contractors.  Because there is often more than one right way to do a job, contractors may suggest additional or alternative approaches to upgrading your home. When you have completed your upgrades, please call us to set up an appointment for a follow-up "E" evaluation.  Your home will be reassessed and given a new, improved rating.  Note that the Total Home Energy Savings Program specifies that an "E" evaluation needs to be completed within 9 months of the date of this evaluation (the day we were at your house). Please keep a record of all upgrades made, including pictures of any insulation upgrades that will be covered when we come back, plus copies of all your receipts

Remember simple things like Air Sealing, Unplugging Electronics or turning off power bars and putting on a sweater rather than turning up the heat are the cheapest and easiest ways of reducing your carbon footprint and saving you money



Thank you for choosing Homesol Building Solutions. We look forward to seeing your improvements,



Homesol Building Solutions Inc.

Atlantic Division

Office: 1-855-489-5305



"Maybe you can't change the whole world, but you can change your corner of it and when enough corners change, the whole world changes."



</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
